F203 — qpi.K12 of a computed expression hashes the wrong bytes

ROOT CAUSE, located at last. Two earlier published causes were wrong; this one is read off the emitted
WAT, which shows the contract compiling *two* K12 instantiations:

    (func $T0_QpiContextProcedureCall_K12 ...
      (call $lh_k12 (local.get $data) (i32.const 8) ...))   <- the two controls
    (func $T1_QpiContextProcedureCall_K12 ...
      (call $lh_k12 (local.get $data) (i32.const 1) ...))   <- the two expressions

`sizeof(T)` is 8 for `qpi.K12(input.a)` and **1** for `qpi.K12(input.a + input.b)`. The argument scratch
is allocated 4 bytes and the value truncated with `i32.wrap_i64` to match. So the expression rows hash
one byte of a truncated copy, which is why the digest is not the K12 of any value in the contract.

`methodArgTypes` in containers.ts recognised three argument shapes — an addressable lvalue, a
construction, and a call naming an aggregate — and returned null for everything else. Every computed
expression is "everything else", so `T` was never bound and `sizeof(T)` fell back to 1.

An rvalue has a type in C++ just as an lvalue does, and for the scalar subset it is the one the usual
arithmetic conversions give. `scalarTypeInfo` already computes exactly that — it is what the backend
trusts to lower the arithmetic itself — so deduction now asks it and maps (width, signedness) back to
the QPI scalar name. General: any `template<typename T>` QPI method called with an expression was
mis-deducing; K12 is only where the width is observable in the answer.

Measured: K12Struct both backends identical, and the invariant the repro asserts now holds —
K12(a) == K12(a*1) and K12(sum) == K12(a+b). Suite 1085/0. The `integers` family is 1451/1451.

